data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_93b0a20b71b47a31006a501523fa108190ac6c1edbf94641c6","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":542,"text":"The capital city of France is Paris.\n\nQuick facts\n- Location: north-central France, on the River Seine.\n- Population: roughly 2.1 million in the city proper; about 12 million in the greater metropolitan area (estimates vary by year).\n- Time zone: Central European Time (CET) / Central European Summer Time (CEST).\n- Language: French. Currency: euro (€).\n\nWhy it’s notable\n- Political and administrative center: seat of the French government and presidency (Élysée Palace), the prime minister (Hôtel Matignon), the French parliament and many national ministries and foreign embassies.\n- Cultural and artistic hub: world-class museums (the Louvre, Musée d’Orsay, Centre Pompidou), major performance venues, and a long history as a center of literature, philosophy and the arts.\n- Fashion and gastronomy: global center for haute couture, luxury goods, cafés, bistros and Michelin-starred restaurants.\n\nKey landmarks and neighborhoods\n- Eiffel Tower, Champs-Élysées and Arc de Triomphe\n- Île de la Cité and Notre-Dame Cathedral (under restoration since the 2019 fire)\n- The Louvre and its pyramid, Musée d’Orsay, Centre Pompidou\n- Montmartre and Sacré-Cœur, Latin Quarter, Le Marais\n- The Seine and its historic quays and bridges (Banks of the Seine are a UNESCO World Heritage site)\n- Stade de France (major sports and concert venue)\n\nTransport\n- Dense public transport network: Métro, RER regional trains, buses and trams.\n- Major rail hubs: Gare du Nord, Gare de Lyon, Gare Montparnasse, etc. High-speed TGV links to the rest of France and neighboring countries.\n- International airports: Charles de Gaulle (CDG), Orly (ORY); Beauvais (for some low-cost carriers).\n\nClimate\n- Oceanic climate: mild winters and warm summers, with moderate rainfall spread through the year.\n\nVisitor tips\n- Paris uses an arrondissement system (20 numbered districts spiraling from the center) that’s helpful for navigation.\n- Popular tourist sites can be very crowded; book major museums and monuments in advance when possible.\n- Standard safety advice: be mindful of pickpockets in crowded areas and on public transport.\n- Tipping: service is typically included on restaurant bills, but leaving a small extra amount for good service is common.\n\nIf you want, I can give a short walking itinerary for a day in Paris, recommend museums by interest, or provide practical info about transport and getting from the airport into the city. Which would you prefer?"}

